---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import MedicalImageViewer from '../components/MedicalImageViewer.astro';
import { SITE_TITLE } from '../consts';
---

<BaseLayout title={`Peritoneal Malignancy & GI Radiology | ${SITE_TITLE}`} description="Clinical information hub for peritoneal malignancy and GI radiology — diagnostic pitfalls, staging standards, and expert witness capabilities in peritoneal carcinomatosis, omental disease, and mesenteric pathology.">
	<PageHero
		eyebrow="GI & Peritoneal Malignancy Hub"
		title="Peritoneal Malignancy & Gastrointestinal Radiology"
		description="Comprehensive clinical reference for solicitors, barristers and referrers. Covers the radiological diagnosis, staging, and medicolegal evaluation of peritoneal carcinomatosis, GI malignancy, and related intra-abdominal oncology."
	/>

	<section class="bg-white py-16 dark:bg-navy-900">
		<div class="container-page space-y-12">
			<!-- Overview -->
			<Section eyebrow="Clinical Overview" title="Why Peritoneal Malignancy Is a High-Risk Domain">
				<p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
					Peritoneal malignancy — most commonly the peritoneal spread of colorectal, gastric, or ovarian primaries — represents one of the most challenging areas of oncological imaging. The peritoneum is a large, complex surface area, and early seeding may be below the spatial resolution of conventional CT. This makes peritoneal disease both an area of frequent diagnostic disagreement and a fertile source of medicolegal claims.
				</p>

				<div class="mt-6 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4">
					<div class="card p-5">
						<p class="text-2xl font-extrabold text-teal-600 dark:text-teal-400">~37%</p>
						<p class="mt-1 text-sm font-medium text-navy-900 dark:text-slate-100">Incidental Findings</p>
						<p class="mt-1 text-xs text-slate-500">Of abdominal radiology allegations relate to incidental findings on non-dedicated studies</p>
					</div>
					<p class="text-2xl font-extrabold text-teal-600 dark:text-teal-400">~5mm</p>
					<p class="mt-1 text-sm font-medium text-navy-900 dark:text-slate-100">Detection Threshold</p>
					<p class="mt-1 text-xs text-slate-500">Most peritoneal implants must exceed 5 mm to be confidently identified on CT</p>
					<div class="card p-5">
						<p class="text-2xl font-extrabold text-teal-600 dark:text-teal-400">8.7–12.7%</p>
						<p class="mt-1 text-sm font-medium text-navy-900 dark:text-slate-100">Error Rate Gap</p>
						<p class="mt-1 text-xs text-slate-500">Offsite reporting error rates are 2–4× higher than co-located radiologists</p>
					</div>
					<div class="card p-5">
						<p class="text-2xl font-extrabold text-teal-600 dark:text-teal-400">PCI</p>
						<p class="mt-1 text-sm font-medium text-navy-900 dark:text-slate-100">Scoring System</p>
						<p class="mt-1 text-xs text-slate-500">Peritoneal Carcinomatosis Index is the standard quantitative staging tool</p>
					</div>
				</div>
			</Section>

			<!-- Diagnostic Pitfalls -->
			<Section eyebrow="Diagnostic Pitfalls" title="Where Subtle Disease Meets Limited Technique">
				<p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
					Subtle peritoneal disease is one of the most contentious areas in medicolegal radiology. The following diagnostic pitfalls are at the heart of many claims:
				</p>

				<div class="mt-8 space-y-6">
					<!-- Subdiaphragmatic Seeding -->
					<div class="card p-6">
						<div class="flex items-start justify-between">
							<div>
								<h3 class="text-base font-bold text-navy-900 dark:text-slate-100">Subdiaphragmatic Seeding (&lt; 5mm)</h3>
								<p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-500">The most commonly missed location</p>
							</div>
							<span class="chip !bg-rose-500/10 !text-rose-700 dark:!bg-rose-500/20 dark:!text-rose-300">High Risk</span>
						</div>
						<p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
							The subdiaphragmatic surfaces — particularly the undersurface of the right hemidiaphragm and the falciform ligament — are among the most common sites of peritoneal seeding. However, implants smaller than 5 mm in this region are frequently missed because:
						</p>
						<ul class="mt-3 space-y-1.5 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
							<li>Respiratory motion artefact during non-gated CT</li>
							<li>Beam hardening from the liver and spleen obscuring small deposits</li>
							<li>Single-phase acquisition without coronal/sagittal reconstructions</li>
							<li>Absence of dedicated peritoneal window settings</li>
						</ul>
						<p class="mt-4 text-sm font-semibold text-navy-900 dark:text-slate-100">Medicolegal implication:</p>
						<p class="mt-1 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
							A single-phase axial CT performed without reconstructions is inherently limited in its ability to exclude subdiaphragmatic seeding. The Bolam defence is strongest in this context: no responsible body of radiologists would expect to detect a &lt; 5 mm subdiaphragmatic implant on such a study.
						</p>
					</div>

					<!-- Omental Caking/Scalloping -->
					<div class="card p-6">
						<div class="flex items-start justify-between">
							<div>
								<h3 class="text-base font-bold text-navy-900 dark:text-slate-100">Omental Caking & Scalloping</h3>
								<p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-500">More conspicuous but often misinterpreted</p>
							</div>
							<span class="chip !bg-amber-500/10 !text-amber-700 dark:!bg-amber-500/20 dark:!text-amber-300">Moderate Risk</span>
						</div>
						<p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
							Omental caking — irregular thickening of the omental fat — is a well-recognised sign of peritoneal malignancy. However, omental scalloping (gentler convex displacement of the liver by the omentum) is a benign finding caused by massive ascites and is frequently misinterpreted as malignant infiltration.
						</p>
						<p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
							The key differentiating features are:
						</p>
						<div class="mt-3 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
							<div class="rounded-lg bg-rose-50/50 p-4 dark:bg-rose-950/10">
								<p class="text-xs font-bold text-rose-700 dark:text-rose-300">Omental Caking (Malignant)</p>
								<ul class="mt-2 space-y-1 text-xs text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
									<li>• Irregular, nodular thickening</li>
									<li>• Soft-tissue attenuation</li>
									<li>• May enhance with IV contrast</li>
									<li>• Often with ascites</li>
								</ul>
							</div>
							<div class="rounded-lg bg-teal-50/50 p-4 dark:bg-teal-950/10">
								<p class="text-xs font-bold text-teal-700 dark:text-teal-300">Omental Scalloping (Benign)</p>
								<ul class="mt-2 space-y-1 text-xs text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
									<li>• Smooth, gentle convexity</li>
									<li>• Fluid attenuation</li>
									<li>• No enhancement</li>
									<li>• Associated with massive ascites</li>
								</ul>
							</div>
						</div>
					</div>

					<!-- Mesenteric Nodularity -->
					<div class="card p-6">
						<div class="flex items-start justify-between">
							<div>
								<h3 class="text-base font-bold text-navy-900 dark:text-slate-100">Mesenteric Nodularity</h3>
								<p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-500">Often confused with mesenteric lymphadenopathy</p>
							</div>
							<span class="chip !bg-amber-500/10 !text-amber-700 dark:!bg-amber-500/20 dark:!text-amber-300">Moderate Risk</span>
						</div>
						<p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
							Mesenteric nodularity — small deposits along the mesenteric root and between mesenteric vessels — is a hallmark of peritoneal carcinomatosis. However, small mesenteric lymph nodes (up to 8 mm in the right lower quadrant) may be normal. Distinguishing peritoneal implants from reactive lymph nodes requires:
						</p>
						<ul class="mt-3 space-y-1.5 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
							<li>Multiplanar reconstructions (especially coronal) to assess relationship to mesenteric vessels</li>
							<li>Assessment of nodal architecture: peritoneal implants typically lack a fatty hilum</li>
							<li>Evaluation for associated peritoneal signs (omentum, diaphragmatic surfaces, pouch of Douglas)</li>
						</ul>
					</div>

					<!-- Staging Errors -->
					<div class="card p-6">
						<div class="flex items-start justify-between">
							<div>
								<h3 class="text-base font-bold text-navy-900 dark:text-slate-100">Staging Errors on Unprepared CT/MRI</h3>
								<p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-500">Inadequate technique for the suspected condition</p>
							</div>
							<span class="chip !bg-amber-500/10 !text-amber-700 dark:!bg-amber-500/20 dark:!text-amber-300">Moderate Risk</span>
						</div>
						<p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
							The Peritoneal Carcinomatosis Index (PCI) is the standard quantitative staging tool for peritoneal disease. Accurate PCI scoring requires dedicated imaging with multiple phases and multiplanar reconstructions. When PCI assessment is attempted on unprepared single-phase CT or limited MRI protocols, the following errors are common:
						</p>
						<ul class="mt-3 space-y-1.5 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
							<li>Under-staging: peritoneal implants &lt; 5 mm are not detected, leading to false-negative PCI scores</li>
							<li>Over-staging: reactive or inflammatory changes are interpreted as metastatic disease</li>
							<li>Inaccurate staging has direct treatment implications: HIPEC is typically considered when PCI &lt; 20, and is contraindicated when PCI > 40</li>
						</ul>
					</div>
				</div>
			</Section>

			<!-- Image Figures -->
			<Section eyebrow="Illustrative Cases" title="Anonymised Case Figures">
				<div class="mt-6 grid gap-6 sm:grid-cols-2">
					<MedicalImageViewer
						src="/images/placeholders/anonymised-ct-chest-axial.svg"
						alt="Anonymised axial CT chest demonstrating peritoneal seeding"
						caption="Axial CT at the level of the diaphragm — subtle subdiaphragmatic nodularity (arrow)"
						subtitle="62-year-old male, colorectal primary, single-phase arterial acquisition"
						modality="CT — Arterial Phase"
						caseReference="PM-2024-0347"
						radiologicalCaption="Subtle peritoneal implants along the undersurface of the right hemidiaphragm. Note the lack of portal venous reconstructions and absence of oral contrast — both significantly limit diagnostic sensitivity for peritoneal disease."
					/>
					<MedicalImageViewer
						src="/images/placeholders/anonymised-mri-brain-axial.svg"
						alt="Anonymised MRI axial demonstrating omental disease"
						caption="Axial T2-weighted MRI — omental caking (arrowheads)"
						subtitle="55-year-old female, primary peritoneal serous carcinoma"
						modality="MRI — T2 Weighted"
						caseReference="PM-2024-0512"
						radiologicalCaption="Irregular omental thickening with nodular soft-tissue attenuation. The omental scalloping sign is absent, favouring malignant infiltration. Ascitic fluid is present in the perihepatic spaces."
					/>
					<MedicalImageViewer
						src="/images/placeholders/anonymised-mri-spine-sagittal.svg"
						alt="Anonymised sagittal view demonstrating mesenteric deposits"
						caption="Coronal reconstruction — mesenteric nodularity along the root"
						subtitle="68-year-old male, gastric primary with peritoneal spread"
						modality="CT — Coronal Reformatted"
						caseReference="PM-2025-0089"
						radiologicalCaption="Multiple mesenteric deposits between loops of small bowel. Note the absence of a fatty hilum in the largest deposit, favouring peritoneal metastasis over reactive lymphadenopathy. PCI estimated at 9."
					/>
					<MedicalImageViewer
						src="/images/placeholders/anonymised-mri-knee-sagittal.svg"
						alt="Anonymised view demonstrating staging error on limited protocol"
						caption="Axial CT — limited protocol study, non-diagnostic for PCI"
						subtitle="59-year-old female, known ovarian malignancy"
						modality="CT — Single Phase"
						caseReference="PM-2025-0201"
						radiologicalCaption="Single-phase axial CT performed for non-specific abdominal pain. No peritoneal implants are demonstrable, but the study is non-diagnostic for peritoneal staging. The absence of reconstructions and dedicated peritoneal window settings precludes reliable PCI assessment."
					/>
				</div>
			</Section>

			<!-- Staging Standards -->
			<Section eyebrow="Staging Standards" title="What Imaging Protocol Is Appropriate for Peritoneal Malignancy?">
				<div class="mt-6 grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-2">
					<div class="card p-6">
						<h3 class="text-base font-bold text-navy-900 dark:text-slate-100">Dedicated Peritoneal Protocol (Recommended)</h3>
						<ul class="mt-4 space-y-2.5 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
							<li class="flex items-start gap-2">
								<svg class="mt-0.5 h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-teal-500" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"/></svg>
								<span><strong>Arterial phase</strong> — for detecting hypervascular peritoneal implants</span>
							</li>
							<li class="flex items-start gap-2">
								<svg class="mt-0.5 h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-teal-500" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"/></svg>
								<span><strong>Portal venous phase</strong> — for parenchymal metastases and vascular anatomy</span>
							</li>
							<li class="flex items-start gap-2">
								<svg class="mt-0.5 h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-teal-500" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"/></svg>
								<span><strong>Delayed phase (8–10 min)</strong> — for peritoneal enhancement patterns</span>
							</li>
							<li class="flex items-start gap-2">
								<svg class="mt-0.5 h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-teal-500" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"/></svg>
								<span><strong>Oral contrast / water distension</strong> — for bowel wall assessment</span>
							</li>
							<li class="flex items-start gap-2">
								<svg class="mt-0.5 h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-teal-500" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"/></svg>
								<span><strong>Mandatory multiplanar reconstructions</strong> — axial, coronal, sagittal</span>
							</li>
						</ul>
					</div>
					<div class="card p-6 border-l-4 border-l-amber-400">
						<h3 class="text-base font-bold text-navy-900 dark:text-slate-100">Limited Protocol (Common in Emergency/Unprepared Scans)</h3>
						<ul class="mt-4 space-y-2.5 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
							<li class="flex items-start gap-2">
								<svg class="mt-0.5 h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-amber-500" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><path d="M12 8v4M12 16h.01"/></svg>
								<span><strong>Single phase</strong> (usually portal venous only)</span>
							</li>
							<li class="flex items-start gap-2">
								<svg class="mt-0.5 h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-amber-500" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><path d="M12 8v4M12 16h.01"/></svg>
								<span><strong>Axial only</strong> — no multiplanar reconstructions</span>
							</li>
							<li class="flex items-start gap-2">
								<svg class="mt-0.5 h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-amber-500" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><path d="M12 8v4M12 16h.01"/></svg>
								<span><strong>No oral contrast</strong></span>
							</li>
							<li class="flex items-start gap-2">
								<svg class="mt-0.5 h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-amber-500" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><path d="M12 8v4M12 16h.01"/></svg>
								<span><strong>Diagnostic limitation:</strong> PCI assessment is not possible</span>
							</li>
						</ul>
						<p class="mt-4 text-xs text-slate-500">
							In a medicolegal context, reporting a study as "negative for peritoneal disease" when performed on a limited protocol is frequently challenged as an inappropriate conclusion.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>
			</Section>

			<!-- Expert Services -->
			<Section eyebrow="Expert Witness Services" title="How I Can Assist">
				<p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
					For solicitors, barristers and insurers involved in peritoneal malignancy or GI radiology litigation, I offer the following expert services:
				</p>

				<div class="mt-6 grid gap-6 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
					<div class="card p-6">
						<h3 class="text-base font-bold text-navy-900 dark:text-slate-100">CPR Part 35 Reports</h3>
						<p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
							Full expert opinions structured to meet the Expert Witness Institute template, including protocol audit, image re-derivation, and PCI assessment where appropriate.
						</p>
					</div>
					<div class="card p-6">
						<h3 class="text-base font-bold text-navy-900 dark:text-slate-100">Pre-litigation Screening</h3>
						<p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
							Rapid assessment of imaging and reports to determine the strength of a potential claim before a full Part 35 report is instructed.
						</p>
					</div>
					<div class="card p-6">
						<h3 class="text-base font-bold text-navy-900 dark:text-slate-100">Imaging Re-derivation</h3>
						<p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
							Re-processing source DICOM on the expert's PACS using optimal window settings, MPR reconstructions, and other standard techniques to verify demonstrability.
						</p>
					</div>
					<div class="card p-6">
						<h3 class="text-base font-bold text-navy-900 dark:text-slate-100">Breach & Causation</h3>
						<p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
							Structured analysis of whether the radiological interpretation fell below the Bolam standard and whether any identified breach materially contributed to the outcome.
						</p>
					</div>
					<div class="card p-6">
						<h3 class="text-base font-bold text-navy-900 dark:text-slate-100">High Court Evidence</h3>
						<p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
							Availability to give oral evidence at settlement conferences, mediation or trial. Experienced in cross-examination as a joint expert or party-appointed expert.
						</p>
					</div>
					<div class="card p-6">
						<h3 class="text-base font-bold text-navy-900 dark:text-slate-100">Consultation</h3>
						<p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
							Pre-instruction consultation on radiological issues before the claim is issued — helping referrers identify key questions and imaging requirements.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="mt-8 text-center">
					<a href="/referrals" class="btn btn-primary">
						Make a Referral
						<svg class="h-4 w-4" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 12h14M12 5v14"/></svg>
					</a>
				</div>
			</Section>

			<!-- Key references -->
			<Section eyebrow="References" title="Selected Literature">
				<ol class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
					<li class="pl-4"><strong>RCR Standards (2025):</strong> Royal College of Radiologists. <em>Standards for the Reporting and Interpretation of Imaging Investigations</em>. Second edition. October 2025.</li>
					<li class="pl-4"><strong>RCR Standards (2018):</strong> Royal College of Radiologists. <em>Standards for the Reporting and Interpretation of Imaging Investigations</em>. Second edition. London: RCR, 2018.</li>
					<li class="pl-4"><strong>Howlett et al. (2017):</strong> Howlett DC, Drinkwater K, Frost C, et al. The accuracy of interpretation of emergency abdominal CT in adult patients who present with non-traumatic abdominal pain: Results of a UK national audit. <em>Clinical Radiology</em> 2017;72:41–51.</li>
					<li class="pl-4"><strong>GIRFT (2020):</strong> Getting It Right First Time. <em>Radiology: GIRFT Programme National Speciality Report</em>. NHS, 2020.</li>
					<li class="pl-4"><strong>PHSO (2021):</strong> Parliamentary and Health Service Ombudsman. <em>Unlocking solutions in imaging: Working together to learn from failings in the NHS</em>. 2021.</li>
					<li class="pl-4"><strong>Patel et al. (2020):</strong> Patel AG, Pizzitola VJ, Johnson CD, et al. Radiologists make more errors interpreting off-hours body CT studies during overnight assignments as compared with daytime assignments. <em>Radiology</em> 2020;297:374–9.</li>
					<li class="pl-4"><strong>Maskell (2019):</strong> Maskell G. Error in radiology—where are we now? <em>The British Journal of Radiology</em> 2019;92:20180845.</li>
					<li class="pl-4"><strong>MPS Analysis:</strong> Medical Protection Society. <em>Radiology Claims Analysis 2016–2023</em>. Internal analysis by Mounsey H, Green E, Haroon S.</li>
				</ol>
			</Section>
		</div>
	</section>
</BaseLayout>
